/T (Frequency Analysis: In longer transmissions, monoalphabetic substitution fails entirely because it preserves the statistical properties of the natural language. In the English language, certain letters appear with highly predictable frequencies \(e.g., 'E' is the most frequent at ~12.7%, followed by 'T', 'A', and 'O'\). By counting the occurrences of characters within an extended ciphertext, an attacker can easily correlate the most frequent ciphertext characters with the known frequency profile of plaintext English. In this brief sample text, the double letters 'II' and 'SS' directly mirror the common linguistic frequency of double vowels and double consonants \(like 'EE' and 'OO'\). Therefore, this cipher fails to provide confidentiality against any semi-skilled adversary.)
/E (Frequency Analysis: In longer transmissions, monoalphabetic substitution fails entirely because it preserves the statistical properties of the natural language. In the English language, certain letters appear with highly predictable frequencies \(e.g., 'E' is the most frequent at ~12.7%, followed by 'T', 'A', and 'O'\). By counting the occurrences of characters within an extended ciphertext, an attacker can easily correlate the most frequent ciphertext characters with the known frequency profile of plaintext English. In this brief sample text, the double letters 'II' and 'SS' directly mirror the common linguistic frequency of double vowels and double consonants \(like 'EE' and 'OO'\). Therefore, this cipher fails to provide confidentiality against any semi-skilled adversary.)
