956 0 obj
<<
/Title (To detect this, forensic investigators apply LSB visual filtration. Tools like StegExpose or custom Python scripts strip away the most significant bits \(which carry the core visual structure of the logo\) and isolate only the least significant bits. If the image is clean, the isolated LSB layer looks like natural, unstructured random noise. If the logo contains hidden data, the LSB filtration reveals distinctive blocks or high- contrast structural patches where data was sequentially injected, exposing a non-random distribution of structural binary data.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 954 0 R
/Next 957 0 R
>>
endobj
957 0 obj
<<
/Title (Methodology C: File Structure and Metadata Integrity Inspection: Many rudimentary data-hiding applications do not inject data directly into the pixel configurations; instead, they append payloads to the file's structural boundaries. Every file format has designated hex markers establishing its boundary rules. A standard PNG file must initiate with the hex header sequence 89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A and conclude with the End-of-Image trailer marker 49 45 4E 44 AE 42 60 82. A standard JPEG starts with FF D8 and terminates with FF D9.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 956 0 R
/Next 955 0 R
>>
endobj
